/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Elements are in series when the same current must pass through each of them: they meet at nodes that nothing else touches.
   - Elements are in parallel when they are connected across the same two nodes, so they share one voltage.
   - A group may be replaced only while it truly shares one current (series) or one voltage (parallel).
   - Voltage divider.
   - Current divider.
   - The divider rules hold only when no current is drawn from the junction between series resistors (an unloaded divider) and when the parallel branches share the stated total current.
   - Inductors combine like resistors, because v = L di/dt adds the same way as v = Ri.
   - Capacitors in series all carry the same charge Q, so V = Q/C: the smaller capacitor takes the larger voltage, which is why the capacitor divider has the other capacitance on top.
*/
