/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 6 μF capacitor C₁ is in series with a parallel pair C₂ = 4 μF and C₃ = 8 μF, across 60 V DC. All start uncharged. Find the equivalent capacitance, the charge drawn from the supply, the voltage on each part and the charge on C₂ and C₃.
   1. Parallel pair adds: C₂₃ = 4 + 8 = 12 μF.
   2. Series with C₁: Ceq = C₁C₂₃/(C₁ + C₂₃) = (6 × 12)/(6 + 12) = 72/18 = 4 μF.
   3. Charge from the supply: Q = CeqV = 4 μF × 60 V = 240 μC. In series, C₁ and the pair each hold this same 240 μC.
   4. Voltages: V₁ = Q/C₁ = 240/6 = 40 V; V₂₃ = Q/C₂₃ = 240/12 = 20 V. Check: 40 + 20 = 60 V. The smaller capacitance took the larger voltage.
   5. Charges in the pair (both at 20 V): Q₂ = 4 μF × 20 V = 80 μC, Q₃ = 8 μF × 20 V = 160 μC; 80 + 160 = 240 μC.
   Answer: Ceq = 4 μF; Q = 240 μC; 40 V on C₁ and 20 V on the pair; Q₂ = 80 μC, Q₃ = 160 μC. */
